course: dictionary
lesson: 1933
title: Strong rules and their clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A commandment is a strong rule that people must keep, not just a suggestion. In the Bible, the Ten Commandments are rules God gave to Moses, such as «Do not steal». The words around the word give you clues: keep, break and forbids all show it is a rule.
explain: Look at this context: «One of the Ten Commandments forbids lying about others.» Forbids tells you the rule says you must not do something. Try a swap: "One of the Ten rules forbids lying about others." It still makes sense, so rule is a good meaning for commandment.
explain: The word also has a wider meaning. «The first commandment says to worship only the one true God.» Here it is the first and most important rule. Outside religion, people may joke about "the eleventh commandment" for a family rule. When you are not sure, check the dictionary.
fact: A commandment is a strong rule or order.
fact: The Ten Commandments are rules God gave to Moses on Mount Sinai, as told in the Bible.
fact: To keep a commandment means to obey it; to break a commandment means to disobey it.
fact: Jesus said the greatest commandments are to love God and to love your neighbour as yourself.
fact: «Commandments» are not only about what we do; Jesus taught they also cover what we want and feel.
exercise: In «One of the Ten Commandments forbids lying about others», what does forbids mean? | Says you must do something every day | Says it is a long story | Says it is a fun game | *Says you must not do something
exercise: Which phrase means to obey the rules? | Break a commandment | *Keep the commandments | Forget the commandments | Carve a commandment
exercise: According to Jesus, what are the greatest commandments? | *To love God and to love your neighbour as yourself | To eat well and sleep early | To count to ten every day | To learn to swim
test: «Jesus said hate in the heart is already the start of murder.» What does this show the commandments are about? | *What we want and feel, as well as what we do | Only what we wear | Nothing at all | Only what we say out loud
test: What does «break a commandment» mean? | Carve a rule in stone | *Disobey a rule | Make up a new rule | Forgive a friend
exam: Which of these is one of the Ten Commandments, as in the Bible? | Never run in the school hall | *Do not steal | Always wear a hat outside | Eat your greens before pudding
